  "notes": [
    {
      "topic": "advising",
      "text": "ACS certification is recommended for students pursuing chemical sciences careers. Requirements differ by option: chemical science option needs CHE 346/CHE 461 or BIO 361; biological chemistry option needs one more chemistry or related elective, CHE 304, and one of several research/lab routes; chemical physics option needs CHE 346/CHE 461 plus 6 additional credits or an approved 180-hour research experience; marine and atmospheric chemistry and environmental chemistry options have their own ACS routes. See the catalog note on ACS certification."
    },
    {
      "topic": "advising",
      "text": "Students who fulfill ACS requirements with an off-campus research experience must register for CHE 487 (0 credits) and prepare a written research report evaluated by a Stony Brook Chemistry faculty member."
    },
    {
      "topic": "advising",
      "text": "Computer programming knowledge is valuable; CHE 358 or another computer programming course is recommended."
    },
    {
      "topic": "other",
      "text": "The Bachelor of Arts in Chemistry is a separate degree with more elective flexibility. Students may also see the Interdisciplinary Program in Engineering Chemistry for materials science combinations."
    },
    {
      "topic": "other",
      "text": "Chemistry Honors Program: students with a cumulative GPA of at least 3.00 in science and mathematics through the junior year are eligible; requires a senior thesis based on senior-year research, an oral examination, and a 3.40 senior-year chemistry GPA for conferral. Honors is optional and not part of the major requirements."
    },
    {
      "topic": "other",
      "text": "Chemistry Secondary Teacher Education Program: see the Education and Teacher Certification entry."
    },
    {
      "topic": "other",
      "text": "B.S./M.S. program in Chemistry: applications at the end of the junior year; includes two 500-level CHE courses, GRD 500, and a fifth-year CHE 599 research sequence. Not evaluated here."
    },
    {
      "topic": "grading",
      "text": "Three major courses may be passed with C- (not identified on the page). G/P/NC grades are not acceptable in courses taken for the major."
